## How navigation works
There's no routing library — `App.js` keeps a single `screen` state variable and
swaps which screen component renders, passing a `navigate(screenName)` function
down as a prop. Each screen calls `navigate("ScreenName")` on button taps. This
keeps the project simple for a group to read and edit without learning
React Navigation.

Tasks are lifted up into `App.js` state (`tasks`, `setTasks`) and passed into
`TasksScreen` and `TaskEditorScreen` so a new task created in the editor shows
up immediately back on the Tasks list.

## Team workflow (per assignment brief)
- Push this whole folder to your **public** GitHub repo (no private repos).
- Each member can safely work on a separate file under `src/screens/` on their
  own branch, then open a Pull Request — screens don't depend on each other's
  internals, only on `navigate` and the shared `tasks` state.
- Everyone needs to run `npm install` once after pulling the repo, since
  `node_modules` should NOT be committed to Git (add a `.gitignore`, see below).

## .gitignore
Create a `.gitignore` file in this folder with:
```
node_modules/
.expo/
dist/
```

